.TH "DOMQUAD.TOJSON" "3JS" "October 30, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
DOMQuad.toJSON \- DOMQuad: toJSON() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]toJSON()\f[B]\f[R] method of the \f[CR]DOMQuad\f[R]
interface returns a JSON\-serializable plain object representing the
\f[CR]DOMQuad\f[R] object.
.PP
The method is automatically called by \f[CR]JSON.stringify()\f[R] when a
\f[CR]DOMQuad\f[R] object is encountered during JSON serialization.
The serialized value can then be deserialized using the
\f[CR]DOMQuad.fromQuad()\f[R] function within the reviver of
\f[CR]JSON.parse()\f[R].
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
toJSON()
.EE
.SS Parameters
None.
.SS Return value
A JSON\-serializable plain object containing the following properties:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]p1\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]p2\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]p3\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]p4\f[R]
.PP
Each property\(cqs value is copied as\-is.
When the returned object is passed to \f[CR]JSON.stringify()\f[R], the
\f[CR]p1\f[R], \f[CR]p2\f[R], \f[CR]p3\f[R], and \f[CR]p4\f[R]
properties are further serialized using
\f[CR]DOMPointReadOnly.toJSON()\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Calling toJSON() directly
This example creates a \f[CR]DOMQuad\f[R] object.
Calling \f[CR]toJSON()\f[R] directly returns a plain object.
Its properties have the same values as the corresponding properties of
the original object.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] topLeft = \f[B]new\f[R] DOMPoint(window.screenX, window.screenY);
\f[B]const\f[R] topRight = \f[B]new\f[R] DOMPoint(
  window.screenX + window.innerWidth,
  window.screenY,
);
\f[B]const\f[R] bottomLeft = \f[B]new\f[R] DOMPoint(
  window.screenX,
  window.screenY + window.innerHeight,
);
\f[B]const\f[R] bottomRight = \f[B]new\f[R] DOMPoint(
  window.screenX + window.innerWidth,
  window.screenY + window.innerHeight,
);

\f[B]const\f[R] quad = \f[B]new\f[R] DOMQuad(topLeft, topRight, bottomRight, bottomLeft);

\f[B]const\f[R] json = quad.toJSON();
console.log(json); \f[I]// A plain object\f[R]
console.log(\f[B]typeof\f[R] json); \f[I]// \(dqobject\(dq\f[R]
console.log(json.p1.x); \f[I]// Same value as quad.p1.x\f[R]
.EE
.SS Serializing to a JSON string
The \f[CR]DOMQuad\f[R] object from the previous example is serialized
using \f[CR]JSON.stringify()\f[R], which calls the \f[CR]toJSON()\f[R]
method automatically.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] quadJSON = JSON.stringify(quad);
console.log(quadJSON);
.EE
.PP
For a window at screen coordinates \f[CR](0, 0)\f[R] with an inner width
of \f[CR]800\f[R] and an inner height of \f[CR]600\f[R], this would log
a JSON string like so (formatted for readability):
.IP
.EX
{
  \(dqp1\(dq: {
    \(dqx\(dq: 0,
    \(dqy\(dq: 0,
    \(dqz\(dq: 0,
    \(dqw\(dq: 1
  },
  \(dqp2\(dq: {
    \(dqx\(dq: 800,
    \(dqy\(dq: 0,
    \(dqz\(dq: 0,
    \(dqw\(dq: 1
  },
  \(dqp3\(dq: {
    \(dqx\(dq: 800,
    \(dqy\(dq: 600,
    \(dqz\(dq: 0,
    \(dqw\(dq: 1
  },
  \(dqp4\(dq: {
    \(dqx\(dq: 0,
    \(dqy\(dq: 600,
    \(dqz\(dq: 0,
    \(dqw\(dq: 1
  }
}
.EE
